    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1331">

                <pb facs="0888" n="2270" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v13p2/pg2270"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Eisenhower
                    Library, Dulles papers, “Telephone
                    Conversations”</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Telephone Conversation Between the
                        Secretary of State and the Deputy Secretary of Defense</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Anderson</hi>)<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d1331-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1954-11-19T08:58:00-05:00"
                            >November 19, 1954—8:58 a.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>The Sec. said he was having a staff meeting on Indochina. Your people—Collins with the strong backing of the JCS—are taking the position that in effect the
                    French have got to be totally phased out from the standpoint of military forces
                    and training. That will be difficult to accomplish but perhaps it can be. The
                    Sec. said he wondered if sufficient thought has been given that if this happens,
                    we take the entire responsibility that the situation will likely be lost. The
                    Sec. is not sure we should be so insistent on sitting alone on this. A. said he
                    is not sure how strong the JCS are on this. They
                    feel not much reliance <pb facs="0889" n="2271" xml:id="pg2271"/> can be placed
                    on the French. The Sec. said that is right, but if we do this, the French will
                    plaster us with the responsibility all over the world and try to sabotage the
                    result—they are still powerful there. If we fail, it will be a terrible blow to
                    our prestige in that area. So far we have been able to say the losses in that
                    area have been French failures. The Sec. said we may have to talk along this
                    line today. A. will talk with Hensel
                    before he comes over here, and A. seemed sympathetic to what the Sec. said.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
